**68%** of Google searches now end without a click, up from 58.5% in 2024 (Conductor, Seer Interactive, SparkToro, 2026). That shift changes what a content marketing statistic is even for. I run content across 44 of my own sites, and the sites still winning aren't the ones publishing the most, they're the ones with something specific enough to get cited when an AI answer engine or a human reader is deciding what to trust. This page uses current 2026 data from HubSpot, the Content Marketing Institute, Orbit Media, First Page Sage and Semrush, checked against what's actually ranking for these queries today, with the caveats most roundups leave out.

## Content Marketing Industry Overview

The market keeps growing even as individual channels get harder to win. Global content marketing revenue is on track to pass **$107.5 billion in 2026**, roughly a third bigger than it was a few years ago. The exact base-year figure varies slightly by source, but the growth trajectory is consistent across every 2026 industry report I checked, so treat the direction as more reliable than the decimal point.

**91%** of B2B marketers now use content marketing in some form, but only **47%** have a documented content strategy (Content Marketing Institute). The strategy gap matters more than the adoption number. Publishing without a documented plan is how a page ends up technically live, technically "content marketing," and invisible in Search Console.

The Content Marketing Institute's 2026 B2B benchmark report, based on 1,015 marketers, found the median B2B company puts about **18%** of its marketing budget into content, with top-performing programmes ranging from **11% to 30%**. Companies investing **25 to 30%** of budget in content generate roughly **3 times** the inbound leads of companies investing under **10%** (CMI, 2026). Budget tends to follow results, not the other way round.

## Can You Actually Trust These Numbers

Not fully, and the numbers explain why. Only around a third of marketers say they can accurately measure content marketing ROI, even though most rank proving ROI as a top priority (Content Marketing Institute, 2026). Sit with that before you take any content marketing statistic, including the ones on this page, as gospel.

It also explains why so many statistics roundups quietly disagree with each other. Ask five sources for "the ROI of content marketing" and you'll get five different numbers, because they're measuring different things over different time periods across different channel mixes. The **748%** average SEO ROI figure from First Page Sage covers a three-year window for search-driven content specifically. The **$7.65** return per $1 spent figure covers content marketing broadly, across every format and channel, most of which don't compound the way search-indexed, evergreen content does. Neither number is wrong. They're answering different questions, and most roundups don't tell you which one you're reading.

My own rule, running content across 44 sites: I don't repeat a stat I can't trace to a named survey with a stated sample size. Every figure on this page is attributed for that reason. Where I couldn't verify a number against a current primary source, I've said so rather than repeat an old one anyway.

## Blog Traffic and the Zero-Click Reality

**85.19%** of blog traffic still arrives through organic search, not social or email (BrightEdge). That figure has circulated across the industry for several years without an obvious more recent replacement, which is worth checking before you rely on it for a 2026 budget decision. Check the publish date on any source repeating it.

What's changed is what "organic search traffic" means once someone actually searches. **68%** of Google searches now end without a click. AI Overviews are Google's AI-generated summary block that sits above the normal results. They now appear on roughly **25%** of searches, up from **13%** in early 2025, and cut organic click-through rate by **61%** when they show up. Brands cited inside an AI Overview see **35%** more clicks than they'd get from a normal listing at the same position (Conductor, Seer Interactive, SparkToro, 2026).

The **35%** citation uplift is the figure that matters for how you write. The traffic available from ranking well hasn't disappeared. It has shifted toward whichever page gets named inside the answer. A statistics page that restates numbers everyone else has already published gives an AI Overview nothing distinctive to cite. A page with sourced, cross-checked, honestly caveated numbers gives it something specific to attribute instead.

## Long-Form Content Performance

![Content length vs performance: 2,000+ word posts deliver 39% strong results vs 21% average](/images/stats/content-length-performance.svg)

Word count for word count, comprehensiveness beats brevity. **39%** of bloggers publishing **2,000+ word** posts report "strong results," against a baseline of **21%** across all bloggers (Orbit Media, 2025 Annual Blogging Survey, roughly 800 respondents). Only **9%** of bloggers write posts that long, which is the opportunity: most published content is still thin, in a search landscape that increasingly rewards depth.

| Word Count | % Reporting Strong Results | % of Bloggers Creating |
|---|---|---|
| Under 500 words | 12% | 4% |
| 500-1,000 words | 18% | 28% |
| 1,000-1,500 words | 21% | 32% |
| 1,500-2,000 words | 29% | 18% |
| 2,000-3,000 words | 36% | 12% |
| 3,000+ words | 39% | 5% |

*Source: Orbit Media Annual Blogging Survey*

The biggest single jump happens between 1,000-1,500 words, the average tier, and 1,500-2,000 words, where reported strong results rise from **21% to 29%**. Average post length itself sits at **1,333 words**, down from 1,416 in 2021. The average keeps falling while the top-performing tier keeps writing more. Watch that gap, not the average.

Longer content correlates with stronger results mainly because it usually means more complete coverage of the topic, not because word count is a ranking factor on its own. A 2,500-word article that covers a subject fully will outrank a padded 3,500-word one. Word count is a proxy for thoroughness. My [content brief service](/services/content-briefs/) sets length targets from actual SERP data rather than a template, so a piece is as long as the topic requires and no longer.

Two more numbers from the same survey are worth knowing before you assume blogging is working for you: only **26%** of bloggers report "strong results" overall, with another **56%** reporting "some" results, and only **29%** say they always use analytics to check what's working. Most people running a blog are guessing at what's landing. If you're not checking Search Console monthly, you're one of them.

## Publishing Frequency: What the Data and Real Results Show

Consistency beats bursts. Orbit Media's data shows bloggers publishing multiple times a week report meaningfully stronger results than weekly or monthly publishers, and the gap compounds: a site publishing steadily for six months builds more authority than one publishing twenty posts in a month and then going quiet.

Real client numbers back this up better than survey averages do. One UK content agency published actual results from clients blogging weekly: one site went from **162 to 2,000 monthly visits in under five months**, another from **1,246 to 10,000 monthly visits within a year**, and a third from **0 to 130,000 monthly visits over three years** (Your Creative Content, published case data). Those are individual case studies, not a controlled sample, so treat them as evidence that consistent publishing can compound rather than as a guarantee for any specific site.

Content decay is the other half of this. HubSpot's analysis found that previously published posts, not new ones, generate **38%** of total blog traffic in a given month. A content calendar that only adds new posts and never revisits old ones leaves that compounding value on the table. Across my own 44 sites, the pages still ranking two years after publication are almost always the ones I've gone back and updated at least once, not the ones I left alone.

## Does AI-Written Content Actually Rank

Not on its own, based on the 2026 data. This is the question I get asked most often right now. Semrush's April 2026 study tracked **42,000 blog posts across 20,000 keywords** and found human-written content holds the #1 Google position **80%** of the time, against **10%** for pure AI-generated content. A separate 16-month tracking study by Digital Applied, covering **4,200 articles**, found AI-only content published without editorial enhancement ranked roughly **23% lower** on average than human-authored content targeting the same keywords, with the gap widening after the March 2026 core update.

The more useful finding is what closes that gap. AI-drafted content that gets substantive human editing, original data and expert attribution performs within **4%** of fully human-written content on median ranking position. The failure mode is using AI as a replacement for editorial judgement rather than as a drafting tool inside it. I use AI to draft and structure content across parts of my own portfolio. Every piece still gets edited against real SERP data, and where I have it, real Search Console evidence, before it goes live. That's the difference the studies are measuring.

## Content Marketing ROI

ROI numbers vary by what's being measured, so here are the two you actually need, kept separate on purpose.

**SEO-driven content**: First Page Sage's 2026 update puts average ROI at **748%** over a three-year period. That's a blended figure across verticals. High-intent categories like real estate sit well above it. Low-consideration categories sit well below it. Treat it as a starting benchmark, not your number. Organic leads close at **14.6%**, against **1.7%** for outbound leads such as cold calling, a gap of roughly **8.5 times** (First Page Sage).

**Content marketing overall**, across every channel and format including the weaker-performing ones, returns **$7.65** for every $1 spent in 2026 (SQ Magazine). That figure sits lower than the SEO-specific one because it includes formats that don't compound the way search-indexed, evergreen content does. If your content programme is mostly one-off social posts and video, don't expect SEO-level returns from it.

Both figures assume the content is strategic: targeted at real search intent, structured for the SERP it's competing in, and maintained rather than abandoned after publication. Publish randomly and you'll land well below either number. My [keyword research service](/services/keyword-research/) identifies the intent and competitive gap behind a query before a single word gets written.

## B2B vs B2C: Budget, Adoption and the Strategy Gap

**91%** of B2B marketers and roughly **70%** of B2C marketers use content marketing as part of their strategy (Content Marketing Institute). The adoption gap between the two has narrowed to the point of being close to irrelevant. Execution hasn't narrowed the same way: only **47%** of B2B marketers have a documented content strategy, meaning most of that 91% is publishing without a written plan.

Budget allocation tells the same story from a different angle. CMI's 2026 B2B report found the highest-performing content programmes allocate **11% to 30%** of total marketing budget to content, median around **18%**, and that businesses investing **25 to 30%** generate roughly **3 times** the inbound leads of businesses investing under **10%**. Budget allocation explains more of that shortfall than channel choice does.

On format, HubSpot's 2026 State of Marketing survey rates short-form video as the top perceived ROI format (**49%** of marketers), ahead of long-form video (**29%**) and live-streaming (**25%**). Written content still ranks among the top five formats for ROI and remains the format most directly responsible for organic search traffic, because it's what search engines and AI answer engines actually index and cite as text. Perceived ROI from a marketer survey and actual search-capture mechanics are two different measurements. Don't cut written content because a survey ranked it below video on a subjective scale.

## What These Statistics Mean for Your Content Strategy

The data points the same way across every section above: content marketing works, most businesses run it without a documented plan or a way to check whether it's working, and the businesses that do both consistently outperform the ones that don't.

**Plan for zero-click, not only for ranking.** With **68%** of searches ending without a click and AI Overviews cutting CTR by **61%** when they show, the goal is being specific enough to get cited, alongside ranking. My [keyword research service](/services/keyword-research/) starts from the intent and competitive gap behind a query, not a keyword list.

**Write to the 2,000+ word tier when the topic actually needs it.** The **39% vs 21%** strong-results gap holds because longer content usually means fuller topic coverage, not because length itself is a ranking factor. A [content brief](/blog/what-is-a-content-brief/) built from real SERP data tells you what "complete" requires for a given query.

**Use AI to draft, not to replace editing.** The **4%** gap between well-edited AI-assisted content and fully human content is achievable. The **23%** gap for unedited AI content is what happens when the editing step gets skipped.

**Build topical authority instead of publishing isolated posts.** [Topical authority](/services/topical-authority/) is what turns a documented strategy into compounding results instead of a calendar with no throughline. [How to build topical authority](/blog/how-to-build-topical-authority/) covers the cluster-based approach.

**Revisit old content on a schedule.** With **38%** of blog traffic coming from previously published posts, a refresh calendar isn't optional maintenance. It's where a meaningful share of your existing traffic already lives.

**Measure it, honestly.** Only around a third of marketers can accurately measure content ROI. If you can't say which pieces are earning their keep in Search Console right now, that's the gap to close before adding more content to the pile.

If you want [to increase organic traffic](/blog/increase-organic-traffic/) from content built to compound, [how long does SEO take](/blog/how-long-does-seo-take/) sets realistic timelines: 3 to 6 months for initial movement, 6 to 12 for real authority. [Semantic SEO](/services/semantic-seo/) is the methodology behind writing content complete enough to rank and get cited, not only published.

